Another type of bag for the game: Portal bags.

Any item you drop into a portal bag disappears inside and is
immediately spat out by another portal bag, which may be elsewhere on
the level or even on a different level.

Ordinarily, the destination bag is randomly generated, so your junk
could end up absolutely anywhere. However, if you (N)ame two (or more)
portal bags the same, then they become linked and an item put into one
has a pretty good chance of being spat out by another same-named bag.
Blessing one or both of the bags improves this chance still further. 
Round up and name enough bags, bless them, place them strategically
and you have a reasonably good little postal system. Obviously a
random "accident" element should be included to keep things
interesting. Stuff might arrive in the wrong place, or get cursed/
polymorphed/ disenchanted along the way, with appropriate B/U/C
modifiers. To balance all these bad effects, maybe (very rare, like 1
in1000) "transporter accidents" could duplicate your item.

Furthermore, like a bag of holding there would be certain items you
wouldn't want to put in there: wands of cancellation would be a bad
idea, bags of holding and other portal bags an even worse idea, the
"oY for obvious reasons, and wands/ rings of teleportation ought to do
crazy things too. I imagine these bags being very popular for getting
heavy corpses across the level to the altar, so fresh corpses put into
portal bags should have a small chance of arriving alive and angry.

This YANI provides a handy way to shift heavy stuff around the dungeon
without trudging through empty levels, but there's always a chance
your stuff will go astray, get mangled and/or get picked up and used
against you before you can get to it. PPl would ppl simply drop their
(estimated) destination bag on an E-word in a locked cupboard, so to
give the monsters a chance to get the stuff, portalled items shouldn't
appear on the same square as the destination bag, but somewhere near
it- you'd want to put it in the middle of a large room to stop your
loot getting embedded in walls. Maybe also have a guaranteed portal
bag held by some big ugly endgame monster, so if you put a nice weapon
or bit of armour into a bag there's a chance that it will be delivered
directly to your worst nightmare...

The coding might be tricky, since it involves manipulating items on
levels other than the current one. However, "bag data" could be stored
in a file and discretely added to the destination level's contents
when that level is reloaded or generated. In the case of
not-yet-generated destinations, the destination level wouldn't be
decided in advance, but each new portal bag generated in a new level
would have a chance of picking up the bag's contents. Not sure what
the implications for bones files are, so maybe portal bags in bones
files should revert to simple sacks.

It would mean a new way to clear out shops as well, but it wouldn't be
that different to walking into a shop with a wand of teleport, so I
don't see that as unbalancing.

And just think of the YASD message for attempting to (W)ear a portal
bag on your head...

Finally, to make the portal harder to ID, consider another new item- a
"bag of emptiness" which simply vanishes anything put into it with a
message identical to that of the portal bag.

unworkable? unbalancing? fun? Comments and opinions encouraged..

